A||||15||Motorhead||Older cars, Mature Women, Vintage Wine!
Z||000000||Motorhead||06-25-99||06:16 PM||||All this heated discussion about 90 something Hondas', and in particular batboys comment concerning 70's musclecars has got me reminiscing.<p>My previous "Loves" have included: '69 Olds 442 convertible, '70 Roadrunner, '70 MGB, '76 Triumph TR 6, '67 Sunbeam Tiger, a couple of Fiat 124 Spyders, and presently a butt kickin' Datsun 280 ZX Turbo, and my beloved '86 Fiero.<p>Each car was lovingly cared for and returned the favor with years of faithful service and enjoyment!<p>Each car also went about it's daily life doing what it was designed to do. The muscle cars went in a straight line very quickly, the sports cars made the long way home more fun!<p>I own older cars for two reasons.  <br>I love restoring them to their original condition, and I HATE passing myself on the road, ie. same make, same model, same year, same color.<p>I loved all my cars equally, and if I had a ten car garage, would still own them all.<p>The purpose of this long winded post is just to say one thing.<p>If you ain't driven it, lived with it, worked on it, loved it or hated owning it, don't bash it!!!<p>The same applies to wine and women, just don't ask me about my ex-wife! <br>
Z||000001||Harry||06-25-99||07:20 PM||||Amen!!!!!
Z||000002||lull777||06-26-99||01:23 AM||||the same applies to women..heh hehh heheh<br>older women or mature women? vintage women?
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||06-26-99||01:58 PM||||Well, being a teenager, I prefer newer cars (however the Fiero seemed ahead of it's time so I definitely include it in "newer" cars..), teenage girls, and root beer.. or iced tea..<p>never really cared much for muscle cars, always prefered the small sporty ones, and I don't drink.. but this is off topic, so I'll be quiet now. :)
Z||000004||Vincent Whitright||06-26-99||05:32 PM||||Hey, I love young women legal to 30, Root Beer( especially fresh...even though bottled soda kind is alright), bottles for my cars(Nitrous), and love many kinds of cars...Fiero's included of course.
Z||000005||Racer 1||06-26-99||08:25 PM||||Oh yes im getting goose bumps just remebering the old days when I was 15 years old.Mid High I got a kawasaki KE 100 motorcycle.Fun to drive low in HP.On my 16 th birthday mamma and pappa introduced me to my first car a 1978 CHEVY IMPALA STATION WAGON.I was almost suicidal at the thought of the chicks laughing at me in high school in the big chevy bucket.But I installed 3 15" woofer in the back to composate for the ugly ride with 20 and below HZ!!I had to struggle thru 2 years in high school with the bucket before upgrading to a 1968 CHEVY Impala 4 door 307 engine and puke green skin!I went thru some good time with this car chick wise.Then 1990 My first fiero with cracked windshield already installed.She was so so so beutiful the fiero.On my way home from the dealer the air intake grill flew off never to be seen again.I still have my black fiero and hopefully will never sell it.Unless someone has a ferrari F50 they want to trade me?  8-) 
Z||000006||Cooter||06-26-99||08:35 PM||||I guess since MotorHead has told us about all his formers loves, I will share mine.<br>The '73 RS Camaro with the hi-po 327 and 4 speed was pretty cool, the '59 European Volkswagon was not! The mighty s-10 truck with its pathetic pea-thrasher motor did not stay with me long. The yellow '68 LWB Chevy truck was a lot of fun back in high school, but it was not cool to drive a paw paw truck to college. So, then came the Nissan Z cars, three of them, one 280z and two 280zx's. The best all around car was probably the '84 z-28, but the gulf war and high gas prices forced it to find a new home, I really miss that car. Then came the '88 CRX, a roller skate on steroids, but a nice man with a lot of money wanted it more than I did, so it also has a new home. The standard hatchback civic was not a replacement for the CRX, so it also found a new home, only to be replaced with my trusty Civic hatchback Si. But one must have a hot rod, so a '79 3/4 ton Suburban followed me home one day. A blueprinted 454 with a Holley projection 900 cfm throttle body adds to the fun. Now, I have started to get into this Fiero thang that everyone loves so much. I have bought and sold a few Fieros in the past few months, but my "keeper" is the '85 se with only 60,000 miles. But I could not leave well enough alone, so I added the fastback clip from a gt to the 4 cyl se. It looks cool and gets a lot of attention. It is a shame that Pontiac threw these cars away because they were really ahead of their time. Thanks for "listening" to my tale of my "old flames". Sorry MotorHead, I am too young to have lived through the Muscle Car era, but I appreciate all the classics, both old and new.
Z||000007||87GT||06-27-99||10:47 PM||||I have only been driving for 4 years now, but I dont think my first one would be considered a love.  My first vehicle was a 88' Plymouth GRAND Voyager.  BLAH!  It had bubbled tint, shag carpet, terrible paint job, and 252,000 miles!  I finally got out of that junk yard and bought my first car which was a 94' Pontiac Grand Prix (I am a pontiac fan).  I really liked this car, it was confortable, and had great A/C.  I had to sell it though because there was a certain fever I caught called, "Fiero Fever."  Im sure some of you know of it.  So that brings me to my GT.  I have had it now since early april and have no problems to report.  Thinking about getting some nice rims, but we'll see. see ya!
Z||000008||batboy||06-28-99||07:06 PM||||Ah yes! I love fast cars and beautiful women (and vice versa). I've owned more vehicles than I can remember, but right now I'm into Fieros, streetrods and older trucks. Currently, I own a 36 Ford truck, 40 Chevy Coupe, 50 Chevy pickup, 68 RS SS 396 Camaro, 70 Chevy pickup with a big block, a rusty ol' Suburban, and two Fieros (a 4 banger and a V8). Someday I'm going to get a 59 Chevy Impala finmobile. **sigh**
Z||000009||Eric||06-29-99||03:55 PM||||Wow!  That's quite a collection.  Where the hell do you keep them?  I wish I could afford to own more than one car at a time.  If I could I'm sure it would be something equally as cool as my GT, but that would be really expensive.  Maybe I'd have to settle with a coupe.
Z||000010||batboy||06-29-99||09:03 PM||||Where do I keep them all. That is a problem. Currently 4 of them have to sit out in the driveway. They are not show cars, all but a couple of them are low budget jobs and 3 of them are nonrunning unfinished projects. I like to drive 'em. Put too much money into them and they become garage babies.<p>By the way, two days ago we had a bad hail storm here (2-3 inch hail stones). Of the 4 vehicles that were sitting outside, my Suburban got a broken windshield and a whole bunch of dents, my 50 Chevy truck got 5 dents in the hood and front fenders. Both of the Fieros were completely undamaged. Viva la plastic!
Z||000011||lowCG||06-29-99||09:11 PM||||Is that a real RS SS?! Pretty rare combo there.<br>The only American car I've owned was the '68 Pontiac Catalina fastback with a 400.<br>My passion has always been light,pumped up european cars(Alfas,Minis,Opel Rally cars,Lancias).Sure is nice to be able to go to the parts store and get the parts you want,like NOW.<br>Always felt that all cars are streetrods,or should be;I'm not a purist(original hose clamps,innertubes,blah),usually wind up re-engineering every car's weakpoints,or emphasizing on their potential in ways that make some people sneer.<br>There's only 2 cars that "got away",that I still wish I'd bought;one,an original,Alfa Guilietta Spider,rust free for $250;and the '68 396SS El Camino,4sp with a really nice(showstopper)orange laquer paint job.(sigh)
Z||000012||Cooter||06-29-99||09:11 PM||||batboy,<br>Did I mention that I work in a body shop? We are very busy right now from a recent hail storm. Free estimates! LOL
Z||000013||batboy||06-30-99||07:41 PM||||LowCG, my Camaro is real, unfortunately though, the numbers don't match on the engine. When my dad bought this car back in the mid 70's, it already had a 327 swapped into it. I've put a 1971 402 big block into it that I rebuilt (the 327 now calls my 1936 Ford truck home). Plus, my dad hated the hideaway headlights. "Another damn thing to go wrong." So, he threw all the motors and vacuum stuff for it away and wired opened the headlights. I'm still trying to locate all that missing crap at swap meets, etc. Very expensive if and when you can find it. Thanks Dad! I need to rebuild the 12 bolt posi rear end next, tore out the ring and pinion gears doing a hole shot awhile back. The ol' gal still needs quite a bit of body work too.
Z||000014||lowCG||06-30-99||09:57 PM||||We've got a 402/TH400 in the '58 Chev Fleetside,caprice clip,tilt wheel,original 411 gears in back,fun setup,for a truck.<br>Sounds like you need a spool for the diff!
Z||000015||batboy||06-30-99||10:32 PM||||Wow, I really like those 58 and 59 trucks. You don't see many 58 Fleetsides around, that was the first year for them. Most of the ones I see are stepsides. Aren't big blocks a lot of fun! I love that torque!<p>I've got all the parts for my Camaro's rear-end, just need to yank it out and haul it to the shop. What happened was it started wheel hopping when I was doing a burn-out. Going to put some nice ladder type traction bars on it before I drive it again.
